e finds working on cars therapeutic.\n  - car stuff: Dave is tied up with car stuff, recently returned from San Francisco with knowledge on car modification. He listens to vinyl while working on cars to relax and stay productive.\n  - everyday stress: Dave finds working on cars, which he does as a job, to be like therapy and a way to get away from everyday stress.\n  - classic cars: Dave opened his car maintenance shop, fulfilling a dream. He works on all kinds of cars, including full restorations of classic cars.\n  - classic muscle car: Dave is working on a classic muscle car, modifying it to have a modern vibe while retaining its classic style. He is learning auto engineering for this project.\n  - Dave: Dave is fascinated by how machines work, starting at age ten after fixing an old car. He enjoys car engineering and transforming broken-down vehicles into high-running ones.\n  - goals: Dave is pursuing his childhood goal of restoring and modifying cars, including adding a custom exhaust and performance upgrades. Calvin offered support to keep pushing for these goals.\n  - automotive engineer: Dave is an automotive engineer whose interest started at age 10 after attending a car show with his dad. He finds building powerful cars from small parts cool.\n  - Dave: Dave is restoring a Ford Mustang from a junkyard, working on car mods, and added a custom exhaust and performance upgrades to it. He attended a car show last Friday.\n  - cars: Dave believes attention to detail in vehicles makes a big impact, echoing his views on music. Calvin relates to stress from car servicing; Dave finds restoring cars fulfilling.\n  - Dave: Dave recently returned from San Francisco with insights on car modification, finding satisfaction in fixing and improving old cars to help others.\n  - dad: Dave's dad took him to his first car show when he was 10, which sparked his interest in cars. Dave later became an automotive engineer.\n  - learning and growing: Dave finds working on cars the