rted by Melanie after a negative experience related to LGBTQ+ issues.\n  - empathy and understanding: Melanie noted that Caroline's empathy and understanding would make her a great counselor, helping people she works with.\n  - Melanie: Melanie congratulated Caroline on passing adoption agency interviews, viewing adoption as a loving way to build a family for needy kids, and encouraged Caroline to be herself.\n  - Melanie (assistant): Melanie enjoyed pottery class yesterday, finding it relaxing and creative. She also complimented Caroline's artwork, noting it captured a peaceful sunset.\n  - people to lean on: Caroline stated that Melanie has people to lean on, which helps during tougher times.\n  - emotions: Melanie has a buddy who adopted a child last year. Caroline is excited about adoption, wanting to provide a safe, loving home.\n  - pottery project: Melanie has a pottery project she is proud of; Caroline encourages her to show it and affirms finding joy is important.\n  - kindness: Caroline remembers the essential nature of being kind and showing support after volunteering at an LGBTQ+ youth center.\n  - pets: Melanie asked Caroline if she has any pets.\n  - mental health: Melanie stated that being yourself is important for mental health and finding peace. Caroline thanked Melanie for the insight.\n  - adoption process: Caroline mentioned an adoption process and Melanie expressed support and asked about what Caroline is excited for regarding it.\n  - Mel: Melanie (Mel) congratulated Caroline on passing adoption agency interviews. She supports Caroline's goal of building a family through adoption to help needy kids.\n  - self-expression and growth: Caroline is inspired by Melanie's pottery project, seeing art as a source of self-expression and growth.\n  - courage to transition: Caroline stated that realizing she could be herself without fear and having the courage to transition was the best part, calling it freeing to express herself authentically with support